  "finalReply": "Created `/app/gates.txt` (1090 gates, < 32000 limit) implementing IEEE CRC32 as an unrolled combinational LFSR:\n\n- `32 = XOR 0 0` (constant 0), `33 = NOT 32` (constant 1)\n- 32 iterations: `feedback = CRC[0] XOR input_bit[k]` (bits in order 0→31 = little-endian LSB-first), `CRC_next[j] = CRC[j+1] XOR (POLY[j] & feedback)` using reflected poly `0xEDB88320`. `POLY[j]=1` → `XOR(old_next, feedback)`; `POLY[j]=0` → `XOR(old_next, 0)` copy.\n- Final 32 gates: `NOT` (final XOR `0xFFFFFFFF`). Wires sequential from 32, so last 32 wires are the CRC output bits, satisfying topological order.\n\nVerified with `sim` vs `binascii.crc32(V.to_bytes(4,'little'))` on 31 values (edges + random): all match, including `0→558161692` and `0xFFFFFFFF→4294967295`.\n\nNote: task example vectors for `1`, `0x12345678`, `42` are inconsistent with the normative `crc32(V.to_bytes(4,'little'))` definition (e.g. `1→0xA505DF1B` is CRC of single byte `0x01`; `0x12345678 hex 0x4A090E98` is CRC of big-endian bytes). Implementation follows the spec definition.",
